=== fractional differentiation (AFML ch.5) ===
intuition: integer d=1 (pct_change) kills memory; fractional d keeps long memory while aiming at stationarity
d=0.4 weight_cutoff_len=200 first5=[ 1.     -0.4    -0.12   -0.064  -0.0416] last=-0.000162677
integer note: when d is a positive integer, weights beyond lag d are exactly 0 → memory cancelled
ADF px level: stat=-2.356 p=0.1545 (usually fail to reject unit root)
ADF fracdiff d=0.4: stat=-5.283 p=0.0000
ADF integer diff d=1: stat=-28.370 p=0.0000
memory_proxy autocorr(lag=20): fracdiff=0.1657 integer_diff=-0.0228
memory_proxy corr(series, px.shift(20)): fracdiff=-0.0481 integer_diff=-0.0613
teach: fracdiff aims for the smallest d that passes a stationarity test while preserving more serial dependence than d=1
chart /workspace/Quant-trading-learn/public/p5/fracdiff_weights.png

=== sample uniqueness / average uniqueness (AFML ch.4) ===
intuition: overlapping label spans are not independent samples — down-weight concurrent events
concurrent_count_by_t: [1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 3, 2, 1]
 t0  t1     y  avg_uniqueness
  0   3  0.02          0.5208
  1   4 -0.01          0.3333
  2   5  0.03          0.2917
  3   4  0.00          0.2500
  4   7 -0.02          0.2917
  5   8  0.01          0.2917
  6   9  0.04          0.2708
  7  10 -0.03          0.2500
  8  11  0.02          0.2500
  9  12  0.01          0.2708
 10  13 -0.01          0.3333
 11  14  0.03          0.5208
equal_weight_mean_y=0.0075 uniqueness_weighted_mean_y=0.0094
sequential bootstrap (1 paragraph): instead of drawing IDs with equal probability, draw the next bootstrap sample with probability proportional to remaining uniqueness given already drawn overlapping events — AFML ch.4 — so bags are less redundant than IID bagging.
reweight_effect: uniqueness weights shrink the influence of highly concurrent (redundant) labels

=== meta-labeling full filter (AFML ch.3) ===
primary side = sign(mom_20); meta learns whether to TRADE or SKIP that side
link: bet sizing after the meta filter → modern-ml-pipelines (P5-02) / AFML ch.10
            config  mean_CS_IC  turnover  net_SR  trade_frac
  A_primary_always      0.0316    0.1636   0.168      1.0000
B_meta_filter_0.52      0.0354    0.3053  -1.455      0.3573
C_meta_filter_0.48      0.0301    0.2303   0.098      0.9481
teach: meta-labeling does not invent the side — it filters when to size the primary bet (→ P5-02 sizing)

=== CPCV reuse note (see P2 backtest-traps for the numeric toy) ===
When you score many purged fold combinations, you get a DISTRIBUTION of OOS metrics — same spirit as AFML ch.12 CPCV. Paths reuse test blocks and are NOT independent DSR trials. Teaching sketch lives in examples/p2/02_backtest_traps.py (cpcv_sr_dist.png).
disclaimer: synthetic weak panel — learning only, not investment advice
